Saturdays are a big day to dine out but  an increasing number of people are  opting to stay home and order in  according to a recent study revenue from  deliveries is up 20 percent in the past  five years with the number of deliveries  up ten percent overall surprisingly the  biggest demand is for breakfast and  lunch now the drive is fueled in large  part by the rise of third-party services  or apps such as GrubHub seamless  ubereats and even doordash they take the  orders online for the restaurants and  deliver the food those restaurants have  taken notice and are even changing the  way they do business from the frying pan  to the front door that's the new mantra  for many diners no matter what they're  eating  just ask bone Appetit editor-in-chief  Adam Rapoport we used to think Pizza  Chinese food that's delivery that has  changed a lot you can pretty much  literally get anything you want  deliver now it's ready to eat like a  restaurant but at home people are still  buying the goods they just want it to  come to them instead of them going to it  mimi blitz is counting on it blitz open  pinky space in New York City's East  Village with co-owner and chef Wesley  waffles earlier this year but don't  expect to sit and eat here it's either  takeout or delivery we wanted to create  happy meals for adults the game was to  have a small footprint in the city so  you know keeps costs down and focusing  on the delivery and the takeout the same  food you could get in a restaurant but  truly in your own exactly yes pinky  space serves up what blitz called a  hybrid of southern and French cuisine  things like brisket and roast turkey are  staples they keep the menu simple to  help and delivery which blitz says is 75  to 80 percent of their business with a  unique space and few setting foot in it  blitzes next goal delivering the  atmosphere along with the food how do  you take this space and bring it to  somebody at home our packaging I think  we've got our packaging here we bring in  a lot of color most restaurants they  just put it in the same kind of to-go  container  and we really wanted to stand out even  more you go into the restaurant you sit  down it's all about the ambience you  know in a way our packaging brings our  ambience to your home the actual  delivery made easier thanks to apps like  seamless GrubHub and ubereats  with most providing not just the  ordering platform but also the delivery  personnel nearly 3,000 miles away in Los  Angeles chef Zach [ __ ] says those  same delivery services are also helping  change the restaurant game to do your  own in-house delivery wood it's a real  undertaking but then suddenly there's  this like option of you know seamless or  caviar they take a commission obviously  but they make your life a little easy  they make your life a little easier and  they allow us to serve a community that  otherwise we just don't have the  resources or the infrastructure or the  experience to do Pollock owns two  restaurants in LA his latest cosa buona  which he designed keeping an evolving  restaurant industry in mind how much of  the business here is delivering about  half of it leaves the front door 4 years  ago when you opened your first  restaurant would you have thought that  delivery would be as important as it is  now I probably would not have when I  opened it I was much more concerned with  you know just the diner's experience  within these four walls as I looked at  like the reality of this industry is not  just a creative process but also a  business delivery became a little bit  more interesting so as a chef he  tailored his menu to better serve the  delivery diner items range from pizza to  clams and octopus the menu is designed  to be delicious whether it hits a table  in 30 seconds or you know hits your  apartment dining room table in an hour  and a half we have like a grilled  broccolini here we put the bread crumbs  on immediately by the time that gets  there they're gonna be soggy and awful  so it's compartmentalized um the bread  crumbs they have the same my crunchy  experience that you would if you were  dining at the restaurant Pollock says  the delivery business has also picked up  at his other restaurant leaving him with  another challenge how is a chef  a restaurant owner do you make sure  people are still coming in and not just  looking to have everything deliver  that's a good question I think that I  think people still go out for a very  different reason people come to  restaurants to you know engage with  friends of theirs to engage peripherally  with strangers to be served I think that  will never be replaced  Bon Appetit Adam Rapoport agrees I think  what the restaurants have going for them  is they've got good lighting good music  they've got vibes  they've got service they got a cocktail  list we're not getting that at home the  difference now is when we're at home  we're eating better than we were before  I'll tell you what it's dangerous for is  dangerous for our pocketbooks ordering  in has become an increasingly fancier  option it's no longer the cheap and easy  thing to do it's easy but it's not cheap  how on earth did they discover that  delivery was in demand it it's the apps  I mean it is just all about the apps  that changed everything both sides it's  easier for you the customer you get  online you don't have to talk to anybody  you order and for them they can mark  things that are no longer offered and  they don't have to worry about the  delivery personality it really has  changed the game I want a list of  cocktails at home
 

